    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_1e8f29207c212ddcc176311b1080f9e0bc5f61adfeeba01bdb799477b701922f",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The two tested attempts to add oscillations fail in different ways: inverse-power perturbations of epsilon_n replace the single-gamma expression by smoother multi-gamma structure while preserving only the leading E log E behavior, and deleting prime-indexed levels changes the logarithmic divergence without improving the reported zero locations. The exact arctangent-sum representation diagnoses a shared obstacle: each level contributes a smooth step on its own energy scale, so these particular modifications have difficulty interpolating the irregular oscillatory count. This supports the source's cautious conclusion that uniform levels seem best within the I model, not a no-go theorem for all inverse models or nonuniform spectra.",
