    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_4dc682231f3e3986445f35f91d3ad19df9108c7aa4e30782f8eb8af98c669208",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Inverting the normally ordered generalized Berry–Keating operator exposes the sign-function kernel of inverse momentum and hence a direct finite-lattice matrix. The diagonal, nonunitary rescaling from an inverse-model eigenstate to a zero-energy Russian-doll state then turns that matrix eigenproblem into a threshold equation; subtracting adjacent threshold equations yields a one-step amplitude transfer ratio, while the two endpoint equations close its product into the exact finite spectral condition. Thus inversion supplies the discretizable representation, the threshold map supplies the solvable representation, and boundary closure recovers the global spectrum that the local recursion alone cannot determine.",
