    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_751fa7b0d05f16fd05c181a585752f03fd8c131c6f093294c0c7c4c6eee77245",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The cumulative coordinate q=integral dn/epsilon(n) both flattens the continuum threshold problem into a twisted free particle and, through s=L_N-q, parametrizes elimination scale. In that shared coordinate the mapped coupling has the state-dependent period 2 pi hbar/E, so one cycle lowers the spectral label, while the exact shifted-lattice product retains finite-size information whose regulated gamma ratio can be tuned to the smooth Riemann count. These relations organize the smooth density and its finite corrections, but they do not produce a Hilbert–Pólya point spectrum: for epsilon(n)=n the box length L_N=log N diverges and the finite-N levels become continuous.",
